    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i was wondering about the vagus nerve specifically though? i heard it has something to do with how we control that parasympathetic response. how does the breathing pattern actually interact with the vagus nerve?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The vagus nerve is a major component of the parasympathetic system, and slow, deep breathing directly stimulates it. As you inhale and exhale slowly through box breathing, this rhythm sends calming signals along the vagus nerve. This stimulation increases vagal tone, which is essentially the measure of your body's ability to relax effectively. Higher vagal tone is associated with better emotional regulation and lower baseline stress.",
      "source": "target",
